WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.930
- Elizabeth Holmes
aspired to be Steve Jobs,

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.870
but it turns out that she's
more like Bernie Madoff.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.270
In 2003, a 19-year-old college dropout

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.840
named Elizabeth Holmes founded
a company called Theranos

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.770
that promised to
revolutionize blood testing.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.140
She claimed to have invented a machine

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.760
that could run more than 200 tests

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.300
on just a few drops of blood
taken from a finger prick.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.210
She wanted to sell diagnostic
tests directly to consumers.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.210
Holmes was very persuasive.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.980
She gave a TED Talk, where
she promoted Theranos

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.920
by calling it the most
disruptive technology

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.500
in the history of medicine.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.200
- Yet, in the United States today,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:36.353
healthcare is the leading
cause of bankruptcy,

00:00:38.078 --> 00:00:43.078
and the lack of it the
leading cause of the suffering

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.880
associated with finding out too late

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.100
in the disease progression process

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.370
that someone you love

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.610
is really, really sick.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.940
- Silicon Valley investors
treated her like a rockstar.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.110
Walgreens quickly became
a corporate partner.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.660
Major investors included Rupert
Murdoch, the Walton family,

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.490
and the family of former
Education Secretary Betsy DeVos.

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:08.580
Holmes' big promises attracted
some of the biggest names

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.360
in American government and foreign policy.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.010
Bill Clinton was charmed by her,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:13.890
and Joe Biden praised her efforts.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.530
Secretaries of State Henry Kissinger

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.970
and George Shultz joined
the board of directors.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.930
Former Secretaries of Defense James Mattis

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:21.320
and William Perry joined, too.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.220
The company raised nearly
$1 billion in funding.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.220
With the prospect of
eliminating many lab tests

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.550
and their associated costs,

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.180
there seemed to be
nothing stopping Theranos

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.810
from becoming a mega player
in the biotech industry.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.970
Its highest valuation was
an eye-popping $9 billion.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.820
But Walgreens, Murdoch, the US
foreign policy establishment,

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.300
and the other investors ignored
a very important principle

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.670
in investing and life.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.470
If something seems too good to be true,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.019
it probably is too good to be true.

00:01:46.019 --> 00:01:49.460
(dramatic music)

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.570
As it turns out, there's
no way to run hundreds

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.540
of sophisticated diagnostic
tests on a few drops of blood.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.060
Despite all the evidence that Holmes knew

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.890
her testing machine would never work,

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.100
a federal jury convicted her on just four

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.580
of the 11 charges against her,

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.400
three for wire fraud against investors,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.890
and one for conspiracy to commit

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.530
wire fraud against investors.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:08.190
One juror said it was
tough to convict her,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.387
because, quote, "She was so likable.

00:02:10.387 --> 00:02:12.560
"She had such a positive dream."

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:13.840
And it's a dream that they think

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.520
she honestly still believes in.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.060
But as you can see,
there's probably a reason

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.590
why Jennifer Lawrence, Katie Holmes,

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.530
and Amanda Seyfried are all practicing

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.560
their most charming
and coldblooded stares.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.600
So today, we're going to talk

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.080
about the Elizabeth Holmes trial.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.190
Why did the jury find her guilty?

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.210
Why did they acquit her
on the other charges?

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.010
And how much did Holmes help herself

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.750
by testifying in her own defense?

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.140
So let's start with the
problems with Theranos,

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.020
which have been
well-chronicled at this point.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.610
Blood analysis is one
of the most important

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:39.630
areas of medicine.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:40.790
The two main players in the field,

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.010
Quest Diagnostics and Laboratory
Corporation of America,

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.490
generate over $75 billion
a year in revenue.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:47.920
Theranos promised to upend this regime

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.300
by eliminating blood draws,
vials, large needles,

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.000
and lengthy waiting periods.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.530
What if you could just go to
Walgreens, buy a testing kit,

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.180
and screen yourself for high cholesterol,

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.060
kidney disease, or even cancer?

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.480
Wouldn't that be great?

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.060
You'd save time, money,
get diagnosed faster,

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.420
and lives would be saved,

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.140
and that was the heart
of the Theranos project.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.600
But there was just one
problem with this vision.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.210
Most blood tests could not be done

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.590
with just a few drops of blood.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.680
There aren't enough
molecules for detection.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.950
One of Holmes' professors
had rejected her early ideas

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:17.210
for biotech companies on the basis

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.870
that the science and
technology could not exist.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.840
The professor, Phyllis Gardner, later said

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.530
that she found the
conversations concerning,

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.020
because Holmes refused to accept

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.110
that some ideas were not possible.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:30.470
She said, quote, "The hubris
of that just drove me insane."

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.340
But Holmes dropped out in
2004, and went on her own way,

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.280
aggressively selling
her ideas to investors.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.980
Her company name Theranos is a combination

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.610
of therapy and diagnostics.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.740
And while Holmes never
submitted any of her inventions

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.780
for any kind of peer
review, that didn't matter

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.980
to another Stanford
professor, Channing Robertson.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:47.080
He said that the minute he heard her idea

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.020
about a diagnostic test from a pin prick,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.287
he felt he was, quote,

00:03:50.287 --> 00:03:53.450
"Looking into the eyes of
Steve Jobs, or Bill Gates."

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:54.900
Steve Jobs was, of course, the person

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.790
that many company founders
wanted to emulate.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.010
Jobs created his own persona,
dressing only in black,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.040
and promoting himself
as an eccentric genius.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.000
Holmes took note of this,
and created her own mythos

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.740
that was trying to piggyback on Jobs.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.400
She wore a black turtleneck
and black suit every day.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.300
She had an intense stare
that became famous,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.840
and we might laugh about this now,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.330
but according to Tyler
Shultz, a Theranos employee,

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.190
investors like his
grandfather, George Shultz,

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.900
were mesmerized by this act.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.180
But then came the whistleblowers.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.260
In 2014, Tyler Shultz
emailed Holmes to complain

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:25.760
that Theranos had doctored research,

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.920
and ignored failed quality control checks.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.960
Several other employees
had also raised concerns

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.140
about the company's science.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.200
George Shultz was 95 at the time,

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.130
and Holmes called him
immediately to warn him

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.130
that she would get revenge on his grandson

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.770
if he took his complaints any further.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.340
Tyler, though, anonymously contacted

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.640
New York State's Public Health Lab,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.200
and filed a complaint alleging
that Theranos had manipulated

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.490
a process known as proficiency testing

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.760
relied on by federal and
state regulators to monitor

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.570
the accuracy of lab tests.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.010
Theranos' lawyer, David Boies,

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:53.640
who later joined the board of directors,

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.610
surprised Tyler at his
grandfather's house,

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.670
informing him that the
company was suing him

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.830
for violating the terms of his NDA.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.640
And while Tyler was the first
person to raise concerns

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:03.250
about Theranos to outside regulators,

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:04.450
he wouldn't be the last.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.220
In 2015, "The Wall
Street Journal" published

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.730
a series of articles exposing

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.740
the Theranos testing machines as frauds.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.610
By then, Holmes had been appointed

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:12.850
to the Harvard Medical Board

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:15.030
alongside her old
professor, Phyllis Gardner.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.450
Gardner spoke with "The
Wall Street Journal"

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:18.230
about her belief that Holmes was a fraud,

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:19.940
and sure enough,
regulators soon discovered

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.130
that Holmes' Edison
machines frequently failed

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:23.630
quality control checks.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.820
That means they produced
unreliable test results.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:28.020
Theranos eventually told
regulators that it voided

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.530
all test results from Edison
machines for 2014 and 2015,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:32.990
as well as some other tests

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:34.720
that ran on conventional machines.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.640
The Edison machine
turned out to be a lemon.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:37.810
Theranos claimed that its test

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.330
for one sexually transmitted
infectious disease was

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.410
sensitive enough to detect
the disease 95% of the time.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.680
But when these results were
submitted for validation,

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.180
it showed sensitivities
of between 65% and 80%.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.750
At the time, Walgreens was
partnering with Theranos

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.460
to provide in-store testing centers

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.430
at 40 Walgreens locations.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.570
But when Walgreens realized
the scope of the problem,

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.960
they sued Theranos for $140 million.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.070
Walgreens eventually
claimed that Theranos voided

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:05.320
11.3% of all blood test reports
that the company provided

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.020
to customers of Walgreens stores.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.960
But instead of acknowledging the problems

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.580
with the Edison machine,
Theranos fabricated the results,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.970
while continuing to market
the company to investors

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:15.740
as world-changing technology.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:17.260
During the trial of Elizabeth Holmes,

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:18.730
prosecutors showed a presentation

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:20.350
that she had made to the
board of directors saying

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.110
that 10 of the 15 largest
pharmaceutical companies

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.920
in the world had validated
the Theranos results.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.480
This was a lie.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.900
The US Centers for Medicare
and Medicaid Services

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.380
investigated Theranos, and
banned Holmes from operating

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:33.880
a medical lab for two years.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.390
The US Securities and Exchange Commission,

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:37.330
the SEC, charged Holmes

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.110
and former Theranos President
Sunny Balwani with fraud,

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.170
and barred Holmes from
serving as a director,

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.180
or officer of a public
company for a decade.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.860
So that brings us to the
criminal case itself.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.530
Holmes was indicted on 11
felony charges in 2018.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:53.040
The charges included wire
fraud against investors,

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.530
wire fraud against
patients, and conspiracy.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.960
And federal prosecutors
painted Holmes as a charlatan

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.840
who knew her technology didn't work.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.010
They said she was obsessed
with fame and fortune,

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.660
and prosecutors presented evidence

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.510
that when Theranos
realized its test results

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.940
were not accurate, the
company substituted tests

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.740
from regular blood draws, making it look

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.960
as if the tests were
from blood droplet tests,

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.490
rather than traditional blood draws

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:14.670
and conventional machines.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:15.890
But the strongest evidence showed

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:18.070
that Holmes exaggerated
the company's involvement

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:19.610
with potential partners.

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:21.500
Prosecutors demonstrated that Holmes lied

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.090
about having deals with drug companies

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.760
like Pfizer and the US military.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:26.500
Holmes even added the logos of Pfizer

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:27.990
and Schering-Plough to lab reports

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.210
that she used in her
presentation to investors

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:31.990
and Walgreens executives.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.670
But these companies
hadn't actually validated

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.470
any of Theranos' systems.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.880
But the defense put on a case as well.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:39.770
Holmes testified for seven days,

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:41.980
presenting herself as a visionary woman

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:43.530
in a male-dominated field.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.700
Holmes told the jury
that she still believed

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.820
that Theranos was on the verge of proving

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:49.180
its technology worked.

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.270
She blamed her ex-partner, Sunny Balwani,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.330
for misleading regulators about the tests.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.150
Holmes told the jury that the two had

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:56.120
secretly lived together,

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:59.230
and that Balwani abused her
throughout the relationship,

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:01.960
and Balwani himself will go
on trial later this year.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.100
And Holmes attempted to
raise a good faith defense

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.150
to the wire fraud charges.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.100
Although good faith is
a defense to wire fraud,

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.140
an honest belief in the ultimate success

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.610
of the enterprise is
not in itself a defense,

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.640
and the judge instructed the
jury that if they believe

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.190
that she acted in good faith
in her representations,

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:18.840
then she could not also have acted

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.030
with an intent to defraud.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:22.190
So let's dig into the
elements of wire fraud

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:25.230
and the conspiracy charges that she faced.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.230
Holmes was eventually
convicted of three counts

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:29.820
of wire fraud and conspiracy
to commit wire fraud.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.630
18 USC 1343 is the wire fraud statute.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:35.217
It states, "Whoever, having
devised, or intending to devise,

00:08:35.217 --> 00:08:37.037
"any scheme, or artifice to defraud,

00:08:37.037 --> 00:08:40.157
"or for obtaining money, or
property by means of false,

00:08:40.157 --> 00:08:43.517
"or fraudulent pretenses,
representations, or promises,

00:08:43.517 --> 00:08:46.497
"transmits, or causes to be
transmitted by means of wire,

00:08:46.497 --> 00:08:48.897
"radio, or television
communication in interstate,

00:08:48.897 --> 00:08:51.910
"or foreign commerce, any
writings, signs, signals,"

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.357
yada, yada, "shall be
fined under this title,

00:08:54.357 --> 00:08:57.130
"or imprisoned not more
than 20 years, or both."

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.490
So the prosecution had
to prove four elements,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.350
first, that she knowingly participated

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.080
in some sort of scheme to
defraud and obtain money,

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.620
or property from other people.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.190
Expressions of opinions,
aspirations, or goals,

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.630
or general subjective claims
can't be used as a basis

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.260
to find that someone made a false,

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:15.940
or fraudulent representation.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.030
This is an important point,

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.610
and one we'll come back to when we talk

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:19.450
about the jury's conclusion

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:21.610
that she didn't defraud consumers,

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.020
probably because some jurors
said that she believed

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:25.650
that she had positive goals.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.610
But the second prong is
that the statements made

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:29.530
as part of the scheme have to be material.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:30.920
Statements tend to be material

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.170
if they have a natural
tendency to influence,

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:34.830
or are capable of influencing a person

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:36.350
to part with their money.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.090
The third prong is that the
government needed to show

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:40.030
that Holmes acted with
the intent to defraud,

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:42.220
that is that she had
the intent to deceive,

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.450
and cheat someone out of
their money, or property.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.540
And then the fourth
prong is that Holmes used

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:48.520
interstate wire communication to carry out

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:49.780
an essential part of the scheme.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.300
And the jury found that
Holmes' misrepresentations

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.550
influenced investors to
send money to Theranos,

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:57.070
and that her presentations
actively intended

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:59.100
to defraud investors
by making them believe

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.210
that Theranos had big government contracts

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:04.170
and private partnerships
that would produce

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.440
a high rate of return on their investment.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.550
And the jury also found
that Holmes had agreed

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.160
with other people to perpetrate
this scheme on others.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.150
That's why she was convicted

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:15.050
of a conspiracy to commit
wire fraud as well.

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.500
The prosecution had lots of evidence

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.990
that Holmes concocted test
results to convince investors

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:21.150
that the science behind
her test was sound.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:24.060
In a 2014 "Forbes" article,
Holmes said that Theranos,

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.070
quote, "Does not buy any
analyzers from third parties,"

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.290
and that was absolutely a lie.

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:30.800
Theranos presented results conducted

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.190
on these third-party analyzers

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:33.920
during their presentations to investors,

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.590
and it's highly likely
that the jury thought

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:36.810
that these lies were material,

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.400
and that they convinced investors
to part with their money.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:41.000
And the three counts represented a total

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.600
of about $142 million in fraud.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.380
But the jury deadlocked
on three additional

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:47.470
wire fraud counts related to investments

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.000
from three Theranos
investors, Alan Eisenman,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.580
Chris Lucas of Black Diamond Ventures,

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:53.760
and Bryan Tolbert of the Hall Group.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:54.970
Prosecutors now have to decide

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:56.440
whether they are going to retry her

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.050
on those three counts,
because the jury hung,

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.360
so they couldn't come to a decision

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:01.610
on those particular counts.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:03.240
But Holmes and Balwani were also accused

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:04.930
of conspiring to commit wire fraud

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.740
in violation of 18 USC 1349.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:09.900
The DOJ charged Balwani
and Holmes with making

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.780
materially false and misleading
statements to investors.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:14.527
The government alleged
that the duo, quote,

00:11:14.527 --> 00:11:16.527
"Made materially false
and misleading statements

00:11:16.527 --> 00:11:19.340
"to investors and failed to
disclose material facts."

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:20.700
This included telling investors

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.280
that the company's proprietary
analyzer was accurate,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.840
when in truth, it had
serious accuracy problems,

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:27.440
performed a limited number of tests,

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:29.520
was slower than some competing devices,

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:32.030
and could not compete with
larger conventional machines

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:35.240
in high-throughput, or the
simultaneous testing of blood

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:36.700
from many patients.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.990
Holmes and Balwani
promised investors a return

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:41.080
on their investment in the
hundreds of millions of dollars,

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.100
while knowing that the
company would only gross

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:47.080
a few hundred thousand
dollars in 2014 and 2015.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.170
Prosecutors proved that
Balwani and Holmes faked

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.500
test results during live
presentations for investors,

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.540
and the DOJ also said
that Holmes told investors

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:57.010
that the Walgreens partnership
would be expanding,

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.010
when in reality, Walgreens
was already moving

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.220
to end the partnership.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:01.850
Balwani and Holmes told investors

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:04.430
that the Theranos technology
had already been deployed

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:07.580
by the US military on the
battlefield in Afghanistan,

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.250
which was untrue.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:11.770
General Mattis testified
that they were not being used

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:12.890
by the military.

00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:14.720
When the jurors spoke
out after the verdict,

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.490
two of them said that
the jury ranked witnesses

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:17.990
according to their believability,

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:19.830
and that they rated Mattis as the witness

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.670
with the highest
credibility, not surprising.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:25.230
Mattis said that he wanted the
military to do a test program

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.940
with Theranos analyzers, but
the tests never happened.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:29.390
Balwani and Holmes told investors

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:31.220
that they didn't need FDA
approval for the machines

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:33.580
and tests when they already
knew that they needed clearance

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:34.700
from the FDA.

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:36.860
And the two also allegedly
lied to the media,

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:39.560
and then used the articles
and news segments containing

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.980
their lies to promote
Theranos to investors.

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.230
And to find Holmes guilty
of the conspiracy charges,

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:46.200
the jury had to believe
that she hatched a plan

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:49.560
to commit wire fraud in partnership
with Balwani, or others.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:51.800
If the jurors thought that she
just went along with Balwani

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.260
because of their partnership,

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:54.710
that probably wouldn't have risen

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:56.290
to the level of conspiracy.

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:57.900
But what about the other charges of fraud

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.520
for crimes perpetrated against patients?

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.820
The jury actually acquitted
Holmes of wire fraud

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:04.760
and conspiracy to defraud patients.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.200
So let's talk about why
that likely happened.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.360
During the four-month trial,
the prosecution devoted

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:12.060
very little time to proving
this part of the case.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:14.800
The patients who purchased
Theranos tests only testified

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.890
for about one hour in total.

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:18.100
The government accused Holmes

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.410
and Theranos of making
material misrepresentations

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:22.560
about its Edison machine
in advertisements.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:24.830
The ads mostly targeted physicians.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.160
The prosecution argued
that these ads induced

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.560
hundreds of patients to
pay Theranos, or Walgreens,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.190
for blood tests and test results,

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.060
which obviously wouldn't have worked.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.450
The government said Balwani
and Holmes told doctors

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:37.910
and patients that Theranos
could provide accurate, fast,

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:40.450
reliable, and cheap blood
tests and test results,

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:42.680
but they already knew that
Theranos technology was

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.130
in fact not capable of
consistently producing accurate

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:46.770
and reliable results.

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:48.360
This was the alleged wire fraud,

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.670
because Theranos used
interstate electronic wires

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:52.360
to purchase advertisements intending

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:55.800
to induce individuals to
buy the tests in Walgreens,

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.120
and stores in California and Arizona.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:00.340
And the prosecution had
evidence that Theranos placed

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.680
advertisements in Walgreens and on TV

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:05.740
with their total marketing
effort slightly over $1 million.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:07.600
But at the same time, the
prosecutors didn't have

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:09.210
the same kinds of direct communications

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.460
between Holmes and individual patients

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:13.800
as they did between Holmes and investors.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:16.040
And theoretically, patients
only used Theranos tests

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:18.420
because they were referred
by their physicians.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.050
So it's possible the
jury might have believed

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:22.410
that there were too many
intermediaries between Holmes

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:23.830
and the individual patients

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:27.270
and doctors to conclude that
Holmes actually influenced

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.460
the patients themselves.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.780
And the defense argued that
the prosecution needed to show

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:33.750
that Holmes really intended
to defraud these patients.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:36.510
Arguably, her company gave
patients inaccurate test results,

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:39.220
but inaccurate test results
alone don't necessarily meet

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:41.780
the criteria for an intent to defraud.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.490
The prosecutors needed to
prove beyond a reasonable doubt

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:46.990
that Holmes knowingly
participated in, devised,

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:49.800
or intended to devise a plan
to defraud these patients.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:50.870
And as we talked about earlier,

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.930
generally, opinions, aspirations, goals,

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.690
or subjective beliefs can't
be used as a basis to find

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:58.450
that someone made a false,
or fraudulent representation.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:00.450
And the jurors who spoke
out after the verdict said

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:01.940
that they believed Holmes when she said

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:03.830
she never intended to defraud patients.

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:06.990
And juror number six, Wayne
Katz, an Emmy-winning TV writer

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:09.740
for a 1990s show called
"Tiny Toon Adventures,"

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:12.150
was inspired by Holmes' testimony.

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:15.337
Quote, "We respected Elizabeth's
belief in her technology,

00:15:15.337 --> 00:15:16.277
"in her dream.

00:15:16.277 --> 00:15:17.877
"We thought, 'She still believes in it,

00:15:17.877 --> 00:15:20.070
"'and we still believe
she believes in it.'"

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.080
He's saying that she
had a subjective belief,

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:24.430
even to this day, that she
was helping her patients,

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:25.310
not hurting them.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:27.770
Katz said that the jury quickly
decided to acquit Holmes

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:29.970
on all four counts of
fraud against patients.

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.520
The jury thought Holmes
was one step removed

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:35.330
from the patients, so that they
were not directly defrauded.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:37.030
Of course, this was also
somewhat contradictory,

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:40.500
since Katz also opined that
everything ran through Holmes.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.130
But this is just what
you often get from jurors

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:44.770
who are grappling with
difficult questions of law.

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:46.480
Mike Kew, juror number 11, said

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.460
that although the panel thought
Holmes' credibility was low,

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:51.200
they also believed that she was genuine.

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:52.370
But what happens next?

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:54.380
Each of the four counts that
Holmes was convicted of carries

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.960
a maximum sentence of 20 years in prison.

00:15:56.960 --> 00:15:59.210
So theoretically, she could
get 80 years in prison,

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:00.690
but this will be reduced

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:03.780
and modified by the federal
sentencing guidelines.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.030
Several former prosecutors think

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:07.470
she'll probably get less
than 20 years in prison,

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:09.330
especially as a first-time offender,

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:11.160
and the high-profile nature of the case

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:12.650
and the scope of the
fraud probably suggest

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:15.010
that she won't exactly get
a slap on the wrist, though.

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:16.910
That's because the federal
sentencing guidelines require

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:19.150
the judge to consider
the magnitude of the lies

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:21.570
and how much money the investors lost,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:23.740
and the fact that she was CEO.

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.550
In these kinds of white collar crimes,

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:28.620
the more money that is pilfered,

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.020
the more prison you could get,

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:33.410
and $140 million is quite a lot,

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:35.350
though Holmes may be
able to sway the judge

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:37.610
for less prison time by
pointing to her relative youth,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.020
her capacity for change,

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:41.890
and the allegations of abuse
at the hands of Balwani,

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:44.470
and there will almost
certainly be a very hefty fine.

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:46.010
The government can retry her

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:48.630
on the other wire fraud charges,
but that seems unlikely.

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:50.940
The government has already
spent significant resources

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:51.920
and time on the case,

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:54.240
and they got a conviction
on four counts carrying

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.030
a serious prison sentence.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.030
So that may be enough,
particularly in light

00:16:58.030 --> 00:16:59.440
of the upcoming Balwani trial,

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:02.940
which is also probably going
to be an expensive prosecution.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.497
So it turns out that Theranos was a fraud

00:17:04.497 --> 00:17:06.100
and a terrible investment.

